# Social typography

Set text in a social image so it can be read on a phone at a glance. Use a short headline, and put the same message in the post text, so people who cannot see the image still get it.

Keep dates, links, readings and instructions in the post text, not only in the picture. [Social colours](colour-social.html) gives the colours for social images.

## Build a hierarchy

Use size and weight to show what to read first. Use 3 styles at most:

| Style | Weight | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Headline | Bold | The main message, such as “See every room’s lights on one card”. No more than 5 lines. |
| Body | Regular | Any longer text. If a headline runs over 5 lines, set it as body text. |
| Label | Bold, smaller than the body | A short tag above the headline, such as “New release” |

Use only Arial regular and bold, as on every Civica surface. [Typeface](typography-typeface.html) explains why.

## Align text

Left-align text by default. A straight left edge is easier to follow, especially for people with dyslexia or low vision.

Centre a headline only if it is short. Do not centre body text, and do not justify it: justified text leaves uneven gaps between words.

## Set line spacing

Use the line heights from the [type scale](typography-type-scale.html): tighter for large headlines, 1.5 for body text. Do not add letter spacing, and do not squeeze lines together to fit more text. Cut the text instead.

## If Arial is not available

Some design tools and platforms do not offer Arial. Use Helvetica instead. If neither is available, use the closest plain sans-serif font with even stroke widths.
